{"subjectId":"sql","id":"subqueries","title":"Subqueries","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":32,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A subquery is a <code>SELECT</code> nested inside another statement. Its result is used as a single value, a list, or a whole table, depending on where it sits.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Returns</th><th>Used in</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Scalar</td><td>One row, one column</td><td><code>SELECT</code>, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>SET</code></td><td><code>salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Row / list</td><td>One column, many rows</td><td><code>IN</code>, <code>ANY</code>, <code>ALL</code></td><td><code>dept_id IN (SELECT …)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Table (derived table)</td><td>Many rows and columns</td><td><code>FROM</code>, <code>JOIN</code></td><td><code>FROM (SELECT …) AS t</code></td></tr><tr><td>Correlated</td><td>Re-evaluated per outer row</td><td><code>WHERE</code>, <code>SELECT</code></td><td>next topic</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number-1\"></i>Scalar subquery</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- employees paid above the company average (93700)\nSELECT name, salary\nFROM employees\nWHERE salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees)\nORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary\n------+-------\nAsha  | 150000\nRavi  | 120000\nPriya | 110000\nMeera | 95000\nKaran | 95000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- scalar subquery in the SELECT list\nSELECT name, salary,\n       salary - (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees) AS diff_from_avg\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 2\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary | diff_from_avg\n------+--------+--------------\nPriya | 110000 | 16300.0\nArjun | 70000  | -23700.0\nNeha  | 72000  | -21700.0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A scalar subquery must return at most one row. PostgreSQL, SQL Server and Oracle raise an error if it returns more; SQLite and MySQL (in some contexts) silently take the first. Zero rows give <code>NULL</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>List subquery: IN, ANY, ALL</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- employees working in a department located in Mumbai or Hyderabad\nSELECT name FROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IN (SELECT dept_id FROM departments WHERE location IN ('Mumbai', 'Hyderabad'))\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n------\nPriya\nArjun\nNeha\nVikram\nSara</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>PostgreSQL / MySQL / SQL Server / Oracle</figcaption><pre><code>-- earn more than EVERY Sales employee / more than ANY Sales employee\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE salary &gt; ALL (SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE dept_id = 2);\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE salary &gt; ANY (SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE dept_id = 2);</code></pre></figure>\n<p>SQLite has no <code>ANY</code>/<code>ALL</code>; rewrite with aggregates — <code>&gt; ALL (…)</code> is <code>&gt; (SELECT MAX(…))</code> and <code>&gt; ANY (…)</code> is <code>&gt; (SELECT MIN(…))</code> (careful: they differ when the subquery is empty or contains <code>NULL</code>s).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, salary FROM employees\nWHERE salary &gt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees WHERE dept_id = 2)\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name | salary\n-----+-------\nAsha | 150000\nRavi | 120000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>Derived table (subquery in FROM)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- average of per-department averages (not the same as the overall average!)\nSELECT ROUND(AVG(dept_avg)) AS avg_of_dept_avgs\nFROM (\n    SELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) AS dept_avg\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\n    GROUP BY dept_id\n) AS per_dept;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>avg_of_dept_avgs\n----------------\n93833.0</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- join a table to an aggregated subquery: each employee vs their department average\nSELECT e.name, e.dept_id, e.salary, ROUND(d.avg_sal) AS dept_avg\nFROM employees e\nJOIN (SELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) AS avg_sal FROM employees GROUP BY dept_id) d\n  ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nWHERE e.salary &gt; d.avg_sal\nORDER BY e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | dept_id | salary | dept_avg\n------+---------+--------+---------\nAsha  | 1       | 150000 | 115000.0\nRavi  | 1       | 120000 | 115000.0\nPriya | 2       | 110000 | 84000.0\nSara  | 3       | 85000  | 82500.0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A derived table needs an alias in most engines (<code>AS per_dept</code>). When a subquery grows or is used twice, promote it to a <strong>CTE</strong> for readability.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between a subquery and a join?</summary><div><p>A join combines columns from both tables into the result; a subquery computes a value/list/table used by the outer query. Many subqueries (<code>IN</code>, <code>EXISTS</code>) can be rewritten as joins and optimizers often do it automatically. Use a join when you need columns from both sides, a subquery for filtering or pre-aggregation.</p></div></details></section>"}